#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# sinh

#% text
# `sinh(x)` gives the hyperbolic sine `(exp(x) - exp(-x))/2`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `sinh(0)` is 0 and `sinh(-x)` is `-sinh(x)`; other exact arguments stay unevaluated. Imaginary arguments give sines: `sinh(im*x)` is `im*sin(x)`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values. `sinh` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
sinh(0)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
sinh(1.0)
#% output
#= 1.1752011936438014

#% cell
sinh(-2.0)
#% output
#= -3.6268604078470186

#% cell
n(sinh(1), 30)
#% output
#= 1.1752011936438014568823818505956008

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Small arguments keep their relative accuracy:

#% cell
sinh(1.0e-10)
#% output
#= 1e-10

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
sinh(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.6349639147847361 + 1.2984575814159773im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(sinh(x), x)
#% output
#= cosh(x)

#% text
# `cosh^2 - sinh^2 = 1`:

#% cell
cosh(1.7)^2 - sinh(1.7)^2
#% output
#= 0.9999999999999991

#% text
# As exponentials:

#% cell
trig_to_exp(sinh(x))
#% output
#= (-1/2)*exp(-x) + (1/2)*exp(x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `cosh`, `tanh`, `sin`, `exp`
